<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lefkandi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lefkandi is an archaeological site on the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcis">Chalcis</a> and Eretria. It was a prosperous settlement in the Early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, when much of Greece was poor and isolated. Its best-known find is a large building of about 950 BCE containing the rich burials of a man and a woman, with horses, showing contacts with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>. It was abandoned around 700 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lefkandi.jpg" alt="Lefkandi" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: C messier · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lefkadi_8074754.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The settlement stood on the promontory locally called Xeropolis, overlooking the Euripos Strait, with small bays to its east and west serving as natural harbours. Its cemeteries lie on the hillslopes to the north-west: the East Cemetery, Skoubris, Palia Perivolia and Toumba, with smaller groups of burials. Occupation goes back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-bronze-age">Early Bronze Age</a>, when a distinct Lefkandi culture existed in parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in Early Helladic IIB (2500–2350 BCE), and the known cemeteries date from about 1050 to 800 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Thick layers of the Late Helladic IIIC period (c. 1200–1100/1075 BCE), excavated in the 1960s, gave archaeologists a pottery sequence for that poorly known age. While many sites in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> were abandoned when the Mycenaean palaces fell, Lefkandi stayed occupied, like Mitrou, Kalapodi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elateia">Elateia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-greece">Central Greece</a>, and its graves show trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>. It is also one of the most representative sites of the Early Protogeometric style.</p><p>In 1980 Evi Touloupa, working with the British School at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, found a large mound covering two shaft graves. One held four horses, apparently sacrificed, two with iron bits still in their mouths. In the other, the ashes of a man, wrapped in fringed linen, lay in a bronze amphora from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> engraved with a hunting scene, beside a sword. Whether the building over the graves was a hero's shrine, and whether it had a wooden veranda, is still debated.</p><p>A woman buried unburnt beside the man wore jewellery, including an electrum ring and a gorget thought to come from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> and to have been a thousand years old at burial, with an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>-handled iron knife near her shoulder. Some scholars think she was killed to accompany the man; others see no clear evidence and regard her as important in her own right.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Lefkandi is now a coastal village of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>. The end of the ancient settlement coincides with growing settlement at nearby Eretria, and the excavators argue that it was Old Eretria, moved farther from Chalkis by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lelantine-war">Lelantine War</a>; some scholars identify it with the ancient city of Argura. The British School at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> has directed the excavations, with campaigns in 1964–1968 and 1981–1984.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lefkandi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Eretria grew on the south coast of Euboea, facing Attica, in the 8th century BCE, possibly succeeding the earlier settlement at <b>Lefkandi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/eretria">Eretria</a></li></ul></section>
